(To Anton:) If you are clinically depressed, eating won't help you.
Trust me, I've been there. They've hypothesized that clinical depression
comes from a genetic predisposition or even determination, something to
do with neurotransmitters (as you said). This rings true because it runs
in my family. However, since I also have a minor dopamine deficiency
(ADD), I have found that taking a neurotransmitter or its precursors
have little to no effect; what works is a drug that stimulates its
production. What you should look into, if this is major, is something
like Prozac (which I took, though not for very long; I didn't care for
it, but people I know swear by it.)

There is, however, a new book out, featured on the cover of a rather
recent (probably August) laissez-faire catalog. It's called _Good Mood:
The New Psychology of Overcoming Depression_. It's written by (I forget
who exactly) an economist who has written for years, and apparently also
struggled with clinical depression for years. The book, as I recall,
outlines ways to deal with, circumvent, and overcome your depressive
symptoms. I've ordered it, but haven't received it yet. If you want to
get it, it's $19.95, at Laissez Faire it's book # ET5817, and LF's fax #
is 415-541-0597. (I'm getting this from my fax order saved to disk.)

Good luck!

