1) What does "and a nit" mean?

2) You're right, that the interests of the species are unrelated to the
issue; anyway, the exact reasons why a squirrel defends itself and
doesn't go squirrelicidal are ultimately irrelevant. My point was that a
squirrel doesn't do so out of any respect for the other squirrel's
rights, as we would (and hopefully do.)


> [A]t what point does 
> reciprocal altruism constitute a recognition of rights in others?
> There's a subtle but important difference between "do unto others
> as they have done unto you" (reciprocal altruism) and "do unto
> others as you would have them do unto you" (a recognition of
> the other's rights, in a sort of over-generalized way, but based on
> confidence in the reciprocal altruism of the other).  That difference
> may just be planning -- and the squirrels might do that, too.

I've never heard those particular definitions, but I'll work with you. A
squirrel (that example may be worn out, time to think of a new
animal...) operates under reciprocal altruism, to a certain extent, as
you said: if the animal were tortured when young, it would probably
snarl at and hide from humans; whereas a squirrel that were raised in a
park surrounded by indifferent, harmless humans and little old ladies
that fed it peanuts, would probably feel very differently towards
humans. A human, under the same circumstances, would probably react the
same way: i.e., become either vicious or friendly. This case, obviously,
does not address rights; but, because rights are two-way, the being to
give rights must first be able to assume that its own rights will be
respected. Either a squirrel or a human that has been molested probably
has little respect for strangers' rights, and therefore would be
reluctant to grant them without specific evidence of their deserving
them. The squirrel, then, would have no problem biting a curious human's
hand, and the (mentally disturbed) human would have no problem gunning
down a McDonald's when depressed. (Or maybe that's extreme...?)

Following it further, if recognition of rights depends on an optomistic
assumption that other beings have rights no matter how you've been
treated, then only an intelligent (i.e., understanding the concept of
rights) being could do such a thing. A squirrel never says (if you've
ever tried to corner one as a child) "Gee, humans are scary, and have
been known to run over my pals, but *this* one may be ok." A human,
however, might say, "Well, my parents used to beat up on me, but I don't
need to resent my boss, since I know that was just an unfortunate
experience--an exception, not the rule." (Barring, of course, severe
emotional trauma.)

Clearly, the difference is not planning, but intelligence and the
ability to objectively think past reciprocal altrusim. This a squirrel
really can't do.


> > This is not to say that a squirrel does not have a right to existence.
> 
> Sure it does.  We might not like killing squirrels, I know I 
> felt awful when I ran over a squirrel earlier this summer, but 
> that's a different issue.  "Right" implies some kind of tort can 
> be applied to redress violation of that right, but I don't think anyone 
> but the squirrel's "similars", if they knew how, should be able to sue or 
> otherwise take revenge because I ran over the squirrel.  (Class action 
> lawsuit with winnings converted to acorns?).

Ok, I'll admit I waffled on the "does the squirrel have rights or
doesn't it?" issue. In the strictest sense of the word, it does not have
rights, including that of a right to existence. I think what I wanted to
do was emphasize that having rights doesn't *necessarily* give us a
privilege to kill off beings that don't have rights. The loss isn't even
comparable, and hunting for sport must be considered, in this sense,
ethical; but I wanted to point out the continuum from dead and very low
life forms (rocks, plants, barnacles) to animals like squirrels, to
primates and pets, to humans, the ones with the rights. The beings
higher on the list are not necessarily better, just more intelligent,
and thus more applicable to our concept of "rights" and being really
"alive", i.e. conscious. This continuum is pretty much objective (since
there's a definite starting point), so if a superintelligence came
along, it would probably not consider us equivalent to barnacles. We'd
be pretty low compared to them, but they'd probably recognize our
potential and spare us out of respect for that.

About "right" implying a tort claim on its violation: the way we've
discussed rights, in order to be grounds for legal action, there would
have to be written agreements--signed with witnesses--between *every*
two individuals, in order to sue for violation of said rights. We've
been discussing them as implicit agreements between two anarchist,
autonomous individuals. A state's conception of rights, however, is
somewhat different. They need to be standardized, and placed outside of
the realm of personal judgement and agrement, and applied to every
member of H. sapiens, which is generally considered a good cutoff point.
Only then can one sue when one's "property rights" are violated. In an
anarchist society, the person would have to a) retrieve the item
semself, if impossible or difficult b) ask for the item back or for
equivalent payment, or c) if the misunderstanding persisted, agree with
the violator on an arbitor. (This assumes rational individuals, however,
which is no small asumption.)


> > A young child doesn't understand
> > property, and so must be given property, and have it defended by, the
> > parents; 
> 
> I'm uncomfortable with the model where parents have an obligation
> to their children, but not vice versa.  With such an imbalance,
> and with society being so paranoid about how people raise their
> children, why would any rational person choose to become a parent? ...
> The whole issue of outsiders interfering with the family (in some 
> cases justified, eg child abuse) is messy, alas.

I didn't mean to imply that the parents had some obligation to their
child. They chose to have the child, and the child is really responsible
(however incabable se may be of responsibility) for semself; either se
will learn to take care of semself, or will not, and who knows what will
happen to the poor kid. The situation is the same with a fetus: the
parent has no obligation to the unborn child. The child, in return, has
no obligation to the parent: since the child is incapable of making an
agreement, anything given to sem by the parents is a gift, and so the
child owes nothing (until se's older, at which point the parents may
demand a deal.)

<phew> think I covered everything, what do you think?

