trees were planted intentionally and East Palo Alto did the same at
the same time, however East PA could not afford the upkeep over the
years and now is almost completely devoid of trees.  

My wife spent 20 years in Houston and she has strong opinions about 
lack of zoning...they have no zoning at all there so that makes for
a good study.  The downside is that someone can buy your neigbor's
property and build a dry cleaners, thus causing your property value
to go down.  Most people consider a neighborhood a "commons" and the
market value of houses, insofar as they can be measured by property
value, is affected by this commons.  So Houston is an example of 
anarchy, and homeowners associations elsewhere in planned communities 
are examples of a market driven approach.


